Project Structure

Your project should look something like this:

phonebook/
│
├── src/
│   ├── App.jsx
│   ├── main.jsx
│   ├── services/
│   │      personService.js
│   └── components/
│          ...
│
├── db.json
└── package.json
Step 1: Create a services Folder

Inside src, create a new folder:

src/
    services/

Inside it, create:

personService.js
Step 2: Move Axios Code

In personService.js:

import axios from "axios";

const baseUrl = "http://localhost:3001/persons";

const getAll = () => {
  return axios.get(baseUrl);
};

const create = (newPerson) => {
  return axios.post(baseUrl, newPerson);
};

export default {
  getAll,
  create,
};

This module now knows how to:

Get all persons.
Create a new person.

Your React component doesn't need to know the URLs anymore.

Step 3: Update App.jsx

Instead of importing axios:

import axios from "axios";

import your service:

import personService from "./services/personService";
Step 4: Replace the GET Request

Instead of:

useEffect(() => {
  axios.get("http://localhost:3001/persons")
    .then(response => {
      setPersons(response.data);
    });
}, []);

write:

useEffect(() => {
  personService.getAll()
    .then(response => {
      setPersons(response.data);
    });
}, []);
Step 5: Replace the POST Request

Instead of:

axios
  .post("http://localhost:3001/persons", personObject)
  .then(response => {
    setPersons(persons.concat(response.data));
  });

write:

personService
  .create(personObject)
  .then(response => {
    setPersons(persons.concat(response.data));
  });
Step 6 (Recommended): Return Only the Data

The Full Stack Open course recommends making the service even cleaner by returning response.data instead of the full Axios response.

Update personService.js:

import axios from "axios";

const baseUrl = "http://localhost:3001/persons";

const getAll = () => {
  return axios.get(baseUrl).then(response => response.data);
};

const create = (newPerson) => {
  return axios.post(baseUrl, newPerson).then(response => response.data);
};

export default {
  getAll,
  create,
};

Now your App.jsx becomes even simpler.

Fetching:
useEffect(() => {
  personService.getAll().then(initialPersons => {
    setPersons(initialPersons);
  });
}, []);
Creating:
personService.create(personObject).then(returnedPerson => {
  setPersons(persons.concat(returnedPerson));
  setNewName("");
  setNewNumber("");
});
Why is this better?

Before:

App.jsx
│
├── UI
├── Event handlers
├── GET request
├── POST request
├── URLs
└── Axios code

Everything is mixed together.

After:

App.jsx
│
├── UI
├── Event handlers
└── Calls personService

personService.js
│
├── GET request
├── POST request
├── URLs
└── Axios code